WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.420
Fans agree: Captain America: Civil War is
one of the best superhero movies ever made.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.010
But just because it's awesome doesn't mean
it's perfect.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:12.380
We're more than willing to forgive Marvel
their few mistakes, but in the spirit of constructive

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.540
criticism, here's a look at some of the dumb
things in Captain America: Civil War that

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:17.990
everyone just ignored.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:19.750
Tony Stark's terrible phone

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:24.660
Product placement in big movies is inescapable
these days, but this is pure marketing shenanigans

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:25.699
run amok.

00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:30.100
In the first Iron Man film, Stark used what
was, at the time, the most cutting-edge phone

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.320
around, the LG VX9400.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:38.280
"Iron Man ability, LG ingenuity, together
in every LG phone."

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:43.960
In Iron Man 2, he got a slate of sci-fi glass
branded with LG and Stark Tech logos.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.380
But in Civil War?

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:50.239
It's a Vivo V3, a fairly cheap phone mainly
available in Asia.

00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:53.410
The move was done for product placement for
when the movie showed in that market.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:55.770
But for a tech billionaire like Stark?

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.030
It just seems silly.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:01.420
He should be using something so cool we can't
even imagine it...not something anyone could

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.059
pick up at a corner shop.

00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:05.470
That security camera on a nowhere back road

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:09.720
Bad guy Helmut Zemo has a sinister plan that
revolves around grainy security footage of

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.270
the Winter Soldier killing Tony Stark's parents.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:17.940
So, two big questions here: why is there a
security camera on a random, deserted backwoods

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.901
road, and how on Earth did the footage survive
to the present day, considering Winter Soldier

00:01:22.901 --> 00:01:25.680
shoots out the camera when he's done?

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.119
It's an amazing coincidence beyond belief
that there just happened to be a security

00:01:29.119 --> 00:01:33.439
camera on a tree right where Howard Stark's
car crashed.

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:36.369
Unless that's the Winter Soldier's lucky killing
tree.

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:39.740
How'd they make that Spidey suit so fast?

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:43.810
Tony Stark convinces Spider-Man to join his
cause in part with the promise of building

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:45.660
him a high-tech new costume.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.649
"You're in dire need of an upgrade.

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:52.119
Systemic, top to bottom, 100 point restoration
that's why I'm here."

00:01:52.119 --> 00:01:55.970
Just a few hours later, Spidey shows up already
wearing the completed suit.

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:57.299
That was fast!

00:01:57.299 --> 00:02:01.490
Has Tony secretly been working on a Spider-Man
suit in his spare time all along?

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:04.850
Maybe Tony's hobby is designing clothes for
all his buddies!

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:08.130
That would explain the Vision's sweaters,
anyway.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.330
That scheme's not what it seems...

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:15.450
Zemo's big plan to cause the Avengers to destroy
themselves works amazingly well, especially

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.590
considering it doesn't actually make any sense
and relies entirely on coincidence.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:23.560
"An empire toppled by its enemies can rise
again.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.080
But one which crumbles from within?

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.560
That's dead."

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:35.220
For starters, there's that unexplained, tree-based
security camera footage we mentioned earlier.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.910
But that's just the start.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:40.730
What about the fact that the big showdown
at the end of the film only works if Iron

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:43.480
Man happens to follow Captain America to the
bunker?

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.170
That's something Zemo couldn't possibly have
known would happen.

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:51.190
If Iron Man had stayed put, the end of the
movie would just have been Cap kicking Zemo's

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:55.400
butt and then stomping the incriminating videotape
into pieces.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:00.760
An even bigger coincidence: his plan is designed
to cause the Avengers to fight amongst themselves.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:05.580
But by the time his plan starts, they are
already fighting with each other over the

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:06.580
Sokovia Accords...

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.510
"Sometimes I wanna punch you in your perfect
teeth."

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.540
Which means his whole plan is kinda...pointless?

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.120
Especially since he could have accomplished
everything he wanted by simply emailing the

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:20.770
video to Tony Stark instead of trying to trick
him into flying all the way to Siberia to

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:22.810
view it on a VHS tape.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:26.610
Hey, Tony could've just watched the video
on his Vivo smartphone!

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.519
Whatever happened to that doomsday virus?

00:03:28.519 --> 00:03:32.580
The movie begins with a breathless action
sequence as the Avengers try to stop the villain

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.890
Crossbones from stealing a deadly biological
weapon.

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:40.349
Just as Captain America is about to learn
who's behind the theft, Crossbones blows himself

00:03:40.349 --> 00:03:41.349
up.

00:03:41.349 --> 00:03:46.630
And then… everyone gets so busy making speeches
they forget to wonder who was hiring supervillains

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:50.770
to steal a doomsday virus and, more importantly,
why.

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:51.770
Was it Zemo?

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:53.510
Or maybe the ghost of Robert Redford?

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:56.210
Or was Ultron up to his old tricks again?

00:03:56.210 --> 00:04:00.939
Maybe we'll learn the answer when everyone
suddenly turns into zombies Avengers 3.

00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:02.810
Where were Nick Fury and SHIELD?

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:07.610
Nick Fury was basically the architect of the
Marvel Cinematic Universe and played a significant

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.730
role in just about every big event within
the MCU.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.629
Well...at least he did.

00:04:12.629 --> 00:04:16.680
Because you'd think he might have something
to say about the events of Civil War.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:20.609
Yet he was completely and mysteriously absent
from the film.

00:04:20.609 --> 00:04:22.650
"Get me off the grid!"

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:23.740
Also missing?

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:27.960
Fury's second in command, Maria Hill, who
we last saw helping train the new super-team

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.660
at the end of Age of Ultron.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:31.210
So what the heck happened to her?

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:35.110
And then there's the total absence of SHIELD,
which has been going strong on television

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.449
even as the movies continue to conspicuously
ignore their existence.

00:04:38.449 --> 00:04:43.090
Given that SHIELD's whole mission at the beginning
of the MCU was to provide oversight to superhero

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:47.630
activities, it would make sense for them to
be at least a little involved with the Sokovia

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:48.630
Accords.

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:53.180
But apparently SHIELD, Nick Fury, and Maria
Hill were all busy taking a long vacation

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:54.180
in Tahiti.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.570
"You should go sometime."

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:56.570
"Where?"

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:57.570
"Tahiti.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.550
It's a magical place."

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:00.610
Flip-floppers

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:06.310
Between Tony Stark's creation, Ultron, blowing
up Sokovia in Avengers: Age of Ultron and

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:11.220
the Scarlet Witch taking out part of a building
in Civil War, the Avengers sure have a lot

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:12.220
to answer for.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:13.220
But not to the government!

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:16.620
Remember, this is the same government that
tried to nuke New York City in Avengers!

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.660
"I got a nuke coming in it's gonna blow in
less than a minute."

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:24.090
It's beyond hypocritical for the government
to get upset at the Avengers for causing collateral

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:28.759
damage when they were planning to kill literally
millions of their own citizens to stop some

00:05:28.759 --> 00:05:29.880
aliens.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:31.229
The recruits don't fit

00:05:31.229 --> 00:05:35.130
When it's time for the big showdown, Captain
America and Iron Man go on recruiting missions.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:38.380
But the folks they end up with don't really
make sense.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:42.610
In Ant-Man, Scott Lang spends the whole film
trying to go straight, leaving the criminal

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:45.270
life behind so he could be a good dad to his
daughter.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:49.320
That goes out the window with no explanation,
as Ant-Man becomes a fugitive again pretty

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:52.310
much because he has a man-crush on Chris Evans.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:54.270
Which… fair enough.

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:55.270
"Wow!

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:56.270
This is awesome!

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:57.270
Captain America.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:58.830
I know you too, you're great!

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:02.900
Jeez."

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:07.060
And that goes double for Hawkeye, who actually
retired at the end of Avengers 2 to spend

00:06:07.069 --> 00:06:12.100
time with his wife and family...only to end
up in a secret prison in the middle of the

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.100
ocean.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.580
Because…well, we don't know why, because
it's never addressed.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:16.580
"Hey Clint."

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:17.580
"Hey man."

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:18.830
"Clearly retirement doesn't suit you.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:20.340
You got tired of shootin golf?"

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:25.410
"Well I played 18, shot 18, just can't seem
to miss."

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:29.330
Meanwhile, on the other side, Tony Stark goes
ahead and recruits a nerdy high school kid

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.490
and throws him into combat against Captain
Freaking America, not to mention the Winter

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:35.960
Soldier, the deadliest assassin the world
has ever known.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:37.560
"You have a metal arm?

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:39.740
That is awesome dude!"

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:41.420
That's just negligent.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:43.050
Aunt May would never be cool with that, Tony.

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:44.850
That's a straight-up dealbreaker, dude.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.870
(Marisa Tomei freaking out on Robert Downey
Jr.)

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:49.009
Thanks for watching!

